{"time": "2026-10-22T05:22:34", "event": "start", "topic": "a bond cash-flow scheduler that computes accrual fractions and adjusted payment dates under named ISDA day-count and business-day conventions (30/360 variants, Actual/Actual ISDA, Modified Following) for a desk's trade file", "model": "z-ai/glm-5.1", "calibration": "Calibration: recent tasks under these rules and how often the reference agent solved them (target: 1-3 of 5 runs).\nPer kind: judgement/property_completeness: 3 task(s), 3/5, 3/5, 3/5\n- http-header-crlf-injection-audit [property_completeness]: 3/5 -> learnable (The task split 3/2 pass/fail because of a critical ambiguity: the instructions didn't clearly specify that CRLF must be stripped/sanitized rather than rejected with 400 errors, so one failing agent rejected requests entirely (breaking test_redirect_no_crlf, test_request_id_no_crlf) while another's sanitization crashed the server (breaking test_clean_redirect_still_works and others). All passing runs correctly stripped CRLF while preserving normal HTTP functionality across redirect, cookie, request-id, and display-name paths. Next design should add an explicit rule distinguishing 'sanitize by stripping' from 'reject with error' and keep the dual-property structure (security + functionality) since the mutation check killed all 5 faults.). Fix all CRLF injection vulnerabilities across a small URL-shortener HTTP service that sets headers through varied code paths and abstraction layers.\n- billing-rounding-consistency [property_completeness]: 3/5 -> too_easy (The billing-rounding-consistency task was well-tested (all 11 mutations killed) but too easy for agents, who passed in only 8\u201310 steps; the 'invalid' outcomes were environment issues rather than difficulty. The next design should keep the strong mutation-killing test coverage but add more independent rules around new billing edge cases (e.g., currency conversion rounding, tax-tier boundaries, multi-item discount interactions) to increase task depth and require more than a quick fix.). Fix a billing module where several code paths round monetary amounts using different methods, violating the round-half-up business rule.\n- docvault-header-cookie-redirect-fix [property_completeness]: 3/5 -> too_easy (All 14 mutation faults (0-13) covering header, cookie, and redirect behaviors were killed by the test suite, confirming strong mutation coverage, and 3 of 5 agents passed by correctly implementing the full set of fixes in 6-10 steps. The 2 invalid runs produced no failed tests, meaning the solutions were structurally rejected before evaluation (likely format or file-targeting issues) rather than functionally wrong. The next design should keep the comprehensive mutation-tested rule set but add a clearer solution contract or scaffold to reduce invalid outcomes, and could branch into independent rules on new themes like authentication flows or response caching.). Fix header injection, open redirect, and cookie injection vulnerabilities across a small HTTP document management service with varied code paths, abstraction layers, and data flows spanning multiple modules.\nTasks solved too often had few rules, rules on one theme, or an oracle to compare against. Make this design harder with more independent, precisely stated rules on more themes and sharper hidden inputs; never by hiding rules. Make it easier than the too_hard ones.", "control": false, "pid": 65034, "genre": "domain"}
